INT. HYPERBARIC CHAMBER - HOSPITAL BASEMENT - NIGHT

A steel cylinder the size of a submarine hatch, riveted, round-windowed, humming. Dials gleam. A single bench runs along each wall. The air smells of hot metal and antiseptic.

DR. LENA ASHWORTH, forties, tall, severe, with grey strands escaping from a tight bun, checks a pressure gauge with the focus of a surgeon.

NURSE PRIYA DESAI, thirties, small, quick, her scrubs covered in a cardigan, adjusts an IV line on a gurney.

MR. WALDEMAR GRUBB, sixties, a diver with the bends, lies on the gurney, bearded, grey-skinned, eyes bright with fear.

LENA
Pressure at two atmospheres. Priya, how is his pulse?

PRIYA
One-ten and climbing.

Lena crosses to Priya. She leans over the gurney. She checks Waldemar's pupils. She frowns and looks up at Priya.

WALDEMAR
Am I dying?

LENA
You are being treated.

WALDEMAR
That is not an answer.

PRIYA
It's the only one she gives.

Lena shoots Priya a look. Priya shrugs. She tucks the blanket tighter around Waldemar's feet.

LENA
Two and a half atmospheres. Watch the oxygen. If he seizes, we have ninety seconds.

PRIYA
(low)
Ninety seconds in a sealed tube. Wonderful.

LENA
I heard that.

WALDEMAR
(wheezing)
Tell me one thing. Which of you two is in charge?

Lena and Priya both begin to answer. Both stop. The dial needle trembles. A rivet pings somewhere in the wall.

PRIYA
She is.

LENA
She is.

They look at each other. Waldemar's whole body arches off the gurney and the alarm shrieks. Lena and Priya grab him at once, one at his shoulders, one at his knees, and hold him down while the chamber hums louder around them.

LENA (CONT'D)
Three atmospheres! Now!

PRIYA
Three! Going up!

She hammers the valve. The needle climbs. Waldemar sags back, gasping, and the alarm cuts out. The sudden silence is enormous.

WALDEMAR
(a whisper)
Thank you.

Lena lets go first. Priya follows. Neither of them speaks.
